        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: Initially, A=[3,2,1,3]A = [3, 2, 1, 3]A=[3,2,1,3].\n\nThe first update sets A1A_1A1​ to 444, so A=[4,2,1,3]A = [4, 2, 1, 3]A=[4,2,1,3] now.\nFor this array, a score of 444 can be obtained as follows:\n\nChoose i=3i = 3i=3, add min⁡(1,3)=1\\min(1, 3) = 1min(1,3)=1 to the score, and then delete 333. The array is [4,2,1][4, 2, 1][4,2,1] now.\nChoose i=1i = 1i=1, add min⁡(4,2)=2\\min(4, 2) = 2min(4,2)=2 to the score, and delete 444. The array is [2,1][2, 1][2,1].\nChoose i=1i = 1i=1, add min⁡(2,1)=1\\min(2, 1) = 1min(2,1)=1 to the score, and delete 222. The array is [1][1][1] and the process ends.\nThe score is 1+2+1=41+2+1 = 41+2+1=4, which is optimal.\n\n\nAfter the second update, the array is [4,2,2,3][4, 2, 2, 3][4,2,2,3] which has a score of 666.\nAfter the third update, the array is [4,2,2,1][4, 2, 2, 1][4,2,2,1] which has a score of 555."
